FINAL ANSWER:
**By commit history, the bus factor of `lib/` is 2.** Troy Goode made 33 of the 66 non-merge commits (50%). Adding the next author, Alejandro Caravaca Puchades (4 commits), takes the total past 50%. In total, 25 people have committed there. The history doesn't call `lib/` a knowledge silo, because no one has more than 80% of the commits.

**By who understands the current code, the risk is much worse.** `lib/` contains a single file, `lib/index.js` (238 lines), and the tool flags it as orphaned. Every author who owns lines in it has had no commits for 6 months, since 2026-04-24:

| Author | Share of lines | Last commit |
|---|---|---|
| "Fiona Formatter" | 89.9% | 2026-04-12 |
| Troy Goode | 8.4% | 2026-04-10 |
| Raymond Feng | 0.8% | 2015 |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 0.4% | 2021 |
| Eric di Domenico | 0.4% | 2013 |

- **Line ownership is mostly an artifact of the reformat.** "Fiona Formatter" looks like the author of the latest commit, "style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes". That commit rewrote nearly every line, so blame credits it with about 90% of the file. It doesn't mean that person designed the code.
- **The real knowledge is probably with Troy Goode.** Troy Goode wrote half the commit history and is also inactive. Counting the two together, the people who understand `lib/` best have gone quiet.
- **Reading the numbers.** Counting only people who are still active, nobody currently owns `lib/index.js`. By that measure it already has no maintainer. By authorship, the practical answer is that losing Troy Goode and one more contributor would leave very little institutional knowledge.

To get a truer picture of who understands the code, I can re-run the blame analysis while ignoring the formatting commit, for example with `git blame --ignore-rev 62628c0`.
